<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fort Ross</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fort Ross was a Russian settlement on the coast of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-american-company">Russian-American Company</a> in 1812. It was meant to grow food for the Alaskan colonies and to hunt sea otters, but it never became profitable and was sold to John Sutter in 1841. It is now a state historic park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fort-ross.jpg" alt="Fort Ross" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frank Schulenburg · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Russian_chapel_at_Fort_Ross_(2016).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fort Ross stands on the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> coast in what is now Sonoma County. The earliest people known at the site lived there in the Upper Archaic period, and the main occupation began about 1500 CE. The area was a large part of Kashaya Pomo territory, which they called Metini, and archaeological data suggest the Kashaya moved there sometime between 1000 and 500 BCE. The Spanish sometimes called the fort the Presidio Ruso.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Russian hunting parties had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> aboard American ships in the early nineteenth century. In 1806 Nikolay Rezanov, a director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-american-company">Russian-American Company</a>, visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> to arrange food supplies for the Alaskan colony, and his captain, Lt. Khvostov, charted the coast to the north and found it unoccupied by other European powers. Rezanov recommended a settlement. On 15 March 1812 Ivan Kuskov arrived at Bodega Bay with 25 Russians and 80 Native Alaskans, and on 11 September 1812 the fortress was dedicated on the name-day of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a>. Four ships were later built there.</p><p>By 1817 sea otters had been practically eliminated from the area. Between 1824 and 1836 Mexican explorers found Russian farms inland from the fort, and Governor José Figueroa gave land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariano-guadalupe-vallejo">Mariano Guadalupe Vallejo</a> to counter the encroachment. By 1839 the settlement's agricultural importance had decreased, and after a trade agreement of 1838 with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudsons-bay-company">Hudson's Bay Company</a> it was no longer needed to feed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>. It was sold in 1841 to John Sutter for 30,000 dollars. Some Russian historians assert that the sum was never paid, while a Sutter biography reports that his agent paid 19,788 dollars in 1849.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The California Historical Landmarks Commission bought the stockade in 1903, and the state received it in 1906. Fort Ross is part of Fort Ross State Historic Park, a California Historical Landmark and a National Historic Landmark. Excavations in 1990 to 1992 identified 135 graves in its cemetery, with the consent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a>, Kashaya Pomo and Alaskan Native associations. The Rotchev House became a house museum in 2010.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fort Ross, California” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The colony depended on imported food and briefly ran <b>Fort Ross</b> in California.</blockquote><a href="/state/russian-america">Russian America</a></li></ul></section>
